2026-06-17 18:35:59,057 DEBUG openai._base_client: Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-fcae59e0-79b9-4af4-8ef4-c0d18cbe79bc', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Analyze this GitHub bounty issue and assess if an AI agent can solve it.\n\nRepository: azerothcore/azerothcore-wotlk\nTitle: Feature: Map Partitioning Support for AzerothCore\nLabels: [\'Feature\']\nBody: ### Describe your feature request or suggestion in detail\n\nHi,\n\nI’d like to propose implementing map partitioning for large continent maps, inspired by the following Project-Epoch TrinityCore PR:\n\n- https://github.com/Project-Epoch/TrinityCore/pull/120\n\n### The Problem\n\nCurrently, all continent maps (like Eastern Kingdoms or Kalimdor) are processed on a single thread. With thousands of entities and players, this becomes a performance bottleneck. While instances benefit from parallel processing via separate map threads, continents remain monolithic, even under heavy load.\n\n### The Proposed Approach\n\nThe PR from TrinityCore introduces a `MapPartitioned` class that allows continents to be split into smaller logical partitions. These partitions share the base map data and are processed in parallel, significantly reducing per-tick load.\n\nNotable points from the implementation:\n\n- Seamless player and entity movement across partitions (no loading screens)\n- Entities are assigned dynamically to the correct partition on startup\n- GridUnload removed to simplify logic and improve performance\n- Shared systems (e.g., weather) remain consistent via the base map\n- Cleaner structure for future improvements and easier debugging\n\n### Benefits for AzerothCore\n\n- Better performance on high-population servers\n- Reduced lag in major cities or crowded quest zones\n- Scales better for events like world PvP or Wintergrasp\n- Lays groundwork for more advanced systems (e.g. phasing, regional load balancing)\n\nI don\'t know if it\'s possible to implement a system similat to this, but this could be a significant architectural upgrade and help AzerothCore scale further as adoption grows. I’d love to hear your thoughts on feasibility and whether a similar system could be integrated.\n\nThanks for your time.\n\n### Describe a possible solution to your feature or suggestion in detail\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_\n\nRate the following (1-10 each):\n- Clarity: How clear is the requirement?\n- Solvability: Can this be solved with code changes alone (no human design decisions)?\n- Scope: Is this small enough to solve in <2 hours of coding?\n\nIf all three scores are >= 7, suggest a brief implementation approach (2-3 sentences).\n\nRespond in JSON:\n{"clarity": N, "solvability": N, "scope": N, "feasible": true/false, "approach": "...", "reason": "..."}'}], 'model': 'nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b', 'max_tokens': 500, 'temperature': 0.2}}
